From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 12:18:15 +0200
Subject: leds: lgm-sso: use the blink engine for blink_set

sso_led_blink_set() programs the hardware blink engine for the pin and
then returns 1. led_blink_setup() reads any non-zero return as a
refusal, so it goes on to led_set_software_blink() and the LED core's
timer drives the lamp instead -- while the engine stays enabled
underneath it. The two then fight over the pin: the timer alternates
brightness between full and LED_OFF, one of those two is always a duty
of 0, and a duty of 0 parks the pin rather than handing it to the duty
engine. So the hardware engine is unreachable through the trigger
interface, and the rate a blinking lamp shows is the core timer's,
jitter and all.

Return 0 once the engine has been programmed, which is what tells the
core to leave the LED alone. Two requests the engine cannot serve keep
falling through to the software timer:

  - Any rank but 0 on a pin below 24. Those pins share a single rate
    field that sso_led_freq_set() does not write, so the rank their
    blink registers already hold is the only rate they can blink at.

  - A duty of 0, which is an LED that is off, or one at full brightness
    on an active-low pin. sso_led_brightness_set() hands the pin to the
    engine only for a non-zero duty, so there is no phase left for the
    engine to drive. The software timer picks a brightness of its own
    and can blink it.

Neither path changes, including the delays being reported back rounded
to the rank the driver settled on, which is what the LED core asks a
blink_set to do when it cannot match a request exactly.

No device tree in tree sets intel,sso-hw-blink, without which the core
is never given a blink_set to call.

Fixes: c3987cd2bca3 ("leds: lgm: Add LED controller driver for LGM SoC")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
index dda4e4fa7980..9644a8433226 100644
--- a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
@@ -351,6 +351,26 @@ sso_led_blink_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev, unsigned long *delay_on,
 	priv = led->priv;
 	freq_idx = delay_to_freq_idx(led, delay_on, delay_off);
 
+	/*
+	 * Only a pin in one of the upper two groups has a rate field of its
+	 * own. Pins 0..23 share one that sso_led_freq_set() does not write, so
+	 * the rank their blink registers already hold is the only rate the
+	 * engine can give them.
+	 */
+	if (freq_idx && sso_led_pin_to_group(led->desc.pin) == LED_GRP0_0_23)
+		return 1;
+
+	/*
+	 * The engine drives the pin from DUTY_CYCLE(pin), and the pairing in
+	 * sso_led_brightness_set() hands the pin over only for a non-zero
+	 * duty: a zero one parks it and there is no phase left to blink. That
+	 * is an LED that is off, or one at full brightness on an active-low
+	 * pin. The software timer picks a brightness of its own and can blink
+	 * such an LED.
+	 */
+	if (!sso_led_duty(led, led->desc.brightness))
+		return 1;
+
 	sso_led_freq_set(priv, led->desc.pin, freq_idx);
 	regmap_update_bits(priv->mmap, SSO_CON2, BIT(led->desc.pin),
 			   1 << led->desc.pin);
@@ -358,7 +378,7 @@ sso_led_blink_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev, unsigned long *delay_on,
 	led->desc.blink_rate = priv->freq[freq_idx];
 	led->desc.blinking = 1;
 
-	return 1;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static void sso_led_hw_cfg(struct sso_led_priv *priv, struct sso_led *led)
